omar (customer): Hey team — hitting repeated 413s when uploading a large NDJSON of embeddings via the streaming endpoint. Works fine for small batches but fails when we send ~45k vectors in one stream. Request ID: r-0a1b2c3d. Any quick ideas?

jessica (support): Sorry you're blocked, omar. Can you paste the exact curl or client code you used and the response headers (including x-request-id) from the 413? Also roughly how big is the payload in MB and how many lines in the NDJSON?

omar (customer): Payload ~95MB, ~45k lines. We used node-fetch streaming to pipe NDJSON file to the /v1/embeddings endpoint. Quick curl repro we used:```bash
curl -v -X POST \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $KEY" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/x-ndjson" \
  --data-binary @big_embeds.ndjson \
  https://api.redwood.example/v1/embeddings
```
Response snippet:```
HTTP/1.1 413 Request Entity Too Large
Content-Type: application/json
x-request-id: r-0a1b2c3d
Retry-After: 0
{
  "error": {"message":"request payload too large","code":413}
}
```

jessica (support): Thanks. Filing as high-priority. Two immediate things: (1) don't retry blindly on 413 — client should split and resend smaller batches; (2) set Content-Encoding: gzip on the upload if you can compress on the client side, that often helps. I'll loop in eng-infra.

liam (eng-infra): looking. 413 means our proxy cut the request due to request size limit, not internal embed runtime. Current nginx proxy limit is 50MB per request in prod (we throttle upstream at the LB). If you have 95MB that's why. We intentionally keep a cap to protect memory.

omar (customer): Ah okay — is there a recommended max vector count or size we should use for streaming? Customers need a guide.

nora (eng-ml): From model/rate perspective we recommend <= 5k vectors per request for stable latency and to avoid KV cache pressure. Practically for NDJSON streaming we recommend keeping requests under ~30–40MB compressed. If using gzip you can often pack ~3x more.

jessica (support): Actionable repro guidance for now: split your NDJSON into chunks of 3k–5k lines, gzip each chunk, and send sequentially. Use exponential backoff on 429, but on 413 reduce payload size instead of retrying. I'll paste a small node streaming snippet we send to users.

alex (support): re: backoff — here's a snippet we recommend (pseudo):```js
async function uploadChunks(chunks){
  for(const chunk of chunks){
    let attempts = 0;
    while(attempts < 5){
      const resp = await fetch(endpoint, {
        method: 'POST',
        headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/x-ndjson', 'Content-Encoding': 'gzip', 'Authorization': `Bearer ${KEY}` },
        body: chunk
      });
      if(resp.status === 201 || resp.status === 200) break;
      if(resp.status === 413){
        // shrink chunk and retry once, don't exponential backoff blindly
        chunk = splitChunk(chunk);
        attempts++;
        continue;
      }
      if(resp.status === 429){
        await sleep(2 ** attempts * 1000); attempts++; continue;
      }
      throw new Error('upload failed ' + resp.status);
    }
  }
}
```

liam (eng-infra): Confirming: we can increase the proxy limit for a dedicated customer under a contract but it's not enabled for hosted users by default. Raising to 100MB globally raises risk vectors. For support we can add a clearer error message to include current max bytes and recommend chunk sizes. I'll open ENGOPS ticket.

jessica (support): Please include logging info so we can map x-request-id -> upstream buffer drop. Right now r-0a1b2c3d shows the proxy blocked it.

nora (eng-ml): One extra trap: when clients stream with Transfer-Encoding: chunked and don't send Content-Length, some proxies still enforce per-request maximum body bytes; chunked doesn't bypass limits. Also watch for newline delimiters — NDJSON must end lines with \n.

omar (customer): Appreciate the details — for now we'll implement chunking + gzip. Is there a recommended default chunk size (MB) to avoid hitting per-request and per-model limits?

nora (eng-ml): Start with 10–20MB gzipped or 2–5k vectors, whichever hits first. That balances throughput vs memory pressure in our runtime. If you need higher throughput, use parallel small uploads (N concurrent requests) rather than huge single streams.

alex (support): Follow-ups: (1) omar — can you share a sanitized sample NDJSON (1–2k lines) to sanity-check format? (2) liam — can you draft the ENGOPS ticket to expose the proxy limit in the 413 body and include x-request-id mapping?

liam (eng-infra): Will do. Ticket: ENGOPS-5219 — add max-content-length to 413 response, and log buffer fill events with request id and client IP. Target: next deploy window.

jessica (support): I'll send omar a recommended client-side helper that does automatic chunking + gzip + adaptive concurrency. Putting stub in a gist and DMing.

omar (customer): Thanks, team — that should unblock us. One last note: we saw intermittent 413 even for compressed chunks ~28MB. I can share the full x-request-ids if that helps.

liam (eng-infra): If you can share those request ids we can correlate with ingress nodes (some nodes had an OOM-protection limiter yesterday). Send them in DMs for privacy.

jessica (support): Marking this thread resolved for now — workaround = chunk + gzip + adaptive concurrency. Reopen if anything else pops up. We'll update docs and post an internal runbook for 413s on embedding uploads. :white_check_mark:

sre-bot: Incident note added to ENGOPS-5219 by liam.

alex (support): Closing loop: added a short KB entry in support playbook: "Embeddings 413s — causes & fixes" with steps and sample code. Link: https://redwood-internal.kb/embeds-413-fixes (internal).
